+        //   as before. That is when an overlay is present (its lengths are the pack-time writer's to know), when the folder cannot be
+        //   written, when the tree changed meanwhile, when this start's managed heap limit does not hold the writer (gclimit.c picks it
+        //   start by start; the directory build below then runs as it did before this patch), and when the tree cannot have an index
+        //   written here: a directory of it is a link, or a name holds a line break or a backslash, which the stamp and the list cannot
+        //   hold. That last refusal is the tree's own, so it is kept (romfs.s2aidx.rbno) and later starts do not walk the tree to find it
+        //   again.
+        //   The order on disk: the stamp first, as pending, so that the folder is known to be writable before the walk and an index always
+        //   has a stamp; then the list and the index; the index flushed to the disk; then the stamp with the index's length, flushed and
+        //   renamed over the pending one. mark: the start mark of the walk (may be null)
+        internal static IStorage RbWriteAndOpen(string romfsDir, string indexPath, string overlayDir, Action<string> mark = null)
